  "notes": "COMMON PATTERNS:\nIchidan verb ({絶|た}える → {絶|た}えます). Intransitive. Very commonly used in the negative form {絶|た}えない (never ceases), which has a positive nuance: {笑|わら}いが{絶|た}えない (always laughing), {客|きゃく}が{絶|た}えない (always busy with customers).\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {連絡|れんらく}が{絶|た}える: contact is lost\n- {笑|わら}いが{絶|た}えない: laughter never stops\n- {客足|きゃくあし}が{絶|た}えない: customers keep coming\n- {家系|かけい}が{絶|た}える: a family line dies out\n- {息|いき}が{絶|た}える: to breathe one's last\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\nThe homophone {耐|た}える means \"to endure\" — a completely different word. {途絶|とだ}える is a near-synonym that emphasizes interruption of something expected to continue.",
